<DocsLayout title="Adoption &amp; updates — sum">
  <div class="doc">
    <h1>Adoption &amp; updates</h1>
    <p class="lede">Adopt the instruction distribution in a separate checkout. Preserve any serving legacy installation, its tasks, unfinished work, and callback paths.</p>
<div class="sec">
<h2>Independent adoption</h2>
<p>Leave the existing legacy checkout at its current commit with installed helper artifacts, task records, worktrees, branches, and callbacks intact. Let its tasks finish or explicitly settle them through their existing trusted procedures.</p><p>Clone the new instructions into a different path. Start new work there from a supported HC-backed Herdr terminal. Carry forward preferences and unresolved decisions as ordinary data; old records confer no native authority.</p>
</div>
<div class="sec">
<h2>Between-session updates</h2>
<p>Inspect and preserve local work, then update the instruction checkout with ordinary Git between sessions. Keep the checkout available while helper briefs reference its absolute procedure paths. Finish or settle active helpers before changing their instructions.</p><p>Merging a PR does not authorize updating a running installation, changing accounts, stopping a shared core, or restarting sessions.</p>
</div>
<div class="sec">
<h2>Manual return and legacy lane</h2>
<p>If needed, manually return to the preserved legacy checkout with its complete compatible procedures for new legacy work. There is no automatic rollback, importer, live cutover, or permanent dual-runtime mode.</p><p>The excluded Grok Bot packs and shared software-factory lane retain their separate compatible legacy target. Do not run that lane against the instruction checkout or reroute its Bot workflows.</p><p><a href={repoBlob("docs/update.md")}> Independent adoption guide ↗</a> <a href={repoBlob("docs/legacy.md")}> Legacy product boundary ↗</a></p>
</div>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
